sections.content:
To demonstrate a practical example of the while loop, we have created a simple "countdown" program:

Example

 int countdown = 3;

while (countdown > 0) {
  cout << countdown 
 << "\n";
  countdown--;
}

cout << "Happy New Year!!\n";

Try it Yourself »

In this example, we create a program that only print even numbers between 0 and 10 (inclusive):

Example

 int i = 0;

while (i <= 10) {
  cout << i << "\n";
  i 
 += 2;
}

Try it Yourself »

In this example we use a while loop to reverse some numbers:

Example

 // A variable with some specific numbers
int numbers = 12345;

// A 
 variable to store the reversed number
int revNumbers = 0;

// Reverse 
 and reorder the numbers
while (numbers) {
  // Get the last number 
 of 'numbers' and add it to 'revNumbers'
  revNumbers = revNumbers * 10 
 + numbers % 10;
  // Remove the last number of 'numbers'

 numbers /= 10;
}

cout << "Reversed numbers: " << revNumbers << "\n";

Try it Yourself »

To demonstrate a practical example of the while loop combined with an if else statement, let's say we play a game of Yatzy:

Example

 int dice = 1;

while (dice <= 6) {
  if (dice < 6) {

 cout << "No Yatzy\n";
  } else {
    cout << 
 "Yatzy!\n";
  }
  dice = dice + 1;
}

Try it Yourself »

If the loop passes the values ranging from 1 to 5, it prints "No Yatzy". Whenever it passes the value 6, it prints "Yatzy!".

code_examples.code:
int countdown = 3;

while (countdown > 0) {
  cout << countdown 
 << "\n";
  countdown--;
}

cout << "Happy New Year!!\n";

sections.content:
When you know exactly how many times you want to loop through a block of 
code, use the for loop instead of a while loop:

Syntax

for (statement 1; statement 2; statement 3) {

   // code block to be executed

 }

Statement 1 is executed (one time) before the execution of the code block.

Statement 2 defines the condition for executing the code block.

Statement 3 is executed (every time) after the code block has been executed.

The example below will print the numbers 0 to 4:

Example

 for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  cout << i << "\n";
}

Try it Yourself »

Example explained

 Statement 1 sets a variable before the loop starts: int i = 0

 Statement 2 defines the condition for the loop to run: i < 5. If the condition is true, the loop will start over again, if it is false, the loop will end.

 Statement 3 increases a value each time the code block in the loop has been executed: i++

This example prints even values between 0 and 10:

Example

for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i = i + 2) {

   cout << i << "\n";

}

Try it Yourself »

This example calculates the sum of numbers from 1 to 5:

Example

int sum = 0;

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {

  sum = sum + i;

}

cout << "Sum is " << sum;

Try it Yourself »

code_examples.code:
Syntax

for (statement 1; statement 2; statement 3) {

   // code block to be executed

 }

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  cout << i << "\n";
}

sections.content:
It is also possible to place a loop inside another loop. This is called a 
nested loop.

The "inner loop" will be executed one time for each iteration of the "outer loop":

Example

 // Outer loop
for (int i = 1; i <= 2; ++i) {

 cout << "Outer: " << i << "\n"; // Executes 2 times

  // Inner 
 loop
  for (int j = 1; j <= 3; ++j) {
    cout << " 
 Inner: " << j << "\n"; // Executes 6 times (2 * 3)
  }
}

Try it Yourself »

code_examples.code:
// Outer loop
for (int i = 1; i <= 2; ++i) {

 cout << "Outer: " << i << "\n"; // Executes 2 times

  // Inner 
 loop
  for (int j = 1; j <= 3; ++j) {
    cout << " 
 Inner: " << j << "\n"; // Executes 6 times (2 * 3)
  }
}

sections.content:
There is also a "for-each loop" (also known as ranged-based for loop), which is 
used to loop through elements in an array (or other data structures):

Syntax

 for (type variableName : arrayName) {
  // code block to be executed

 }

The following example outputs all elements in an array, using a "for-each loop":

Example

 int myNumbers[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
for (int num : myNumbers) {

 cout << num << "\n";
}

Try it Yourself »

You can also use a for-each loop to loop through characters in a string:

Example

string word = "Hello";

for (char c : word) {

  cout << c << "\n";

}

Try it Yourself »

Note: Don't worry if you don't understand the examples above. You will learn more about arrays in the C++ Arrays chapter.
Good to know: The for-each loop was introduced in C++ version 11 (2011).

code_examples.code:
Syntax

 for (type variableName : arrayName) {
  // code block to be executed

 }

int myNumbers[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
for (int num : myNumbers) {

 cout << num << "\n";
}

sections.content:
To demonstrate a practical example of the for loop, let's create a program that counts to 100 by tens:

Example

 for (int i = 0; i <= 100; i += 10) {
  cout << i << "\n";
}

Try it Yourself »

In this example, we create a program that only print even numbers between 0 and 10 (inclusive):

Example

 for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i = i + 2) {
  cout << i << "\n";
}

Try it Yourself »

Here we only print odd numbers:

Example

 for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i = i + 2) {
  cout << i << "\n";
}

Try it Yourself »

In this example we print the powers of 2 up to 512:

Example

 for (int i = 2; i <= 512; i *= 2) {
  cout << i << "\n";
}

Try it Yourself »

And in this example, we create a program that prints the multiplication table for a specified number:

Example

 int number = 2;
int i;

// Print the multiplication table for the 
 number 2
for (i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  cout << number << " x " << i 
 << " = " << number * i << "\n";
}

Try it Yourself »

You have already seen the break statement used in an earlier chapter of this tutorial. It was used to "jump out" of a switch statement.

The break statement can also be used to jump out of a 
loop.

This example jumps out of the loop when i is equal to 4:

Example

 for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  if (i == 4) {

 break;
  }
  cout << i << "\n";
} 

Try it Yourself »

The continue statement breaks one iteration (in the loop), if a specified condition occurs, and continues with the next iteration in the loop.

This example skips the value of 4:

Example

 for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  if (i == 4) {

 continue;
  }
  cout << i << "\n";
} 

Try it Yourself »

You can also use break and continue in while loops:

Break Example

 int i = 0;
while (i < 10) {
  cout << i << "\n";
  i++;

   if (i == 4) {
    break;

 }
} 

Try it Yourself »

Continue Example

 int i = 0;
while (i < 10) {
  if (i == 4) {
    i++;
    continue;

   }
  cout << i << "\n";
  i++;
} 

Try it Yourself »

