If you want to add elements at the time of declaration, place them in a comma-separated list, inside curly braces {}:

Example

 // Create a set called cars that will store strings

 set<string> cars = {"Volvo", "BMW", 
 "Ford", "Mazda"};

// Print set elements

 for (string car : cars) {
  cout << car << "\n";
}

 The output will be:

BMW

Ford

Mazda

Volvo

Try it Yourself »

As you can see from the result above, the elements in the set are 
sorted automatically. In this case, alphabetically, as we are working with strings.

If you store integers in the set, the returned values are sorted numerically:

Example

 // Create a set called 
 numbers that will store integers

 set<int> numbers = {1, 7, 3, 2, 5, 9};

// Print set elements

 for (int num : numbers) {
  cout << 
 num << "\n";
}

